---
// English version of src/pages/experiences/daikoku-tours-jdm.astro (adapted, not translated word for word).
// Clément hasn't been to Daikoku (weather): everything was checked online on 2026-11-04, sources in the FR file.
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../../../components/Hero.astro";
import Icon from "../../../components/Icon.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
import EncartReserver from "../../../components/EncartReserver.astro";
import MentionAffiliation from "../../../components/MentionAffiliation.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../../consts";
import heroImg from "../../../assets/photos/tokyo-nuit-rx7-veilside.jpg";

const pageOffers = [
  "onlyjdm-location",
  "daikoku-gyg-car-culture",
  "daikoku-gyg-tokyo-turismo",
  "daikoku-klook-nuit",
  "daikoku-klook-au-volant",
];
const mapsHref = `https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=${encodeURIComponent("Daikoku Futo Parking Area, Yokohama")}`;

const faq = [
  {
    q: "Can you get to Daikoku by train or on foot?",
    a: "No. The parking area sits on the urban expressway, so you need a car: your own, a tour's, or a taxi. I wouldn't count on a taxi though: nothing guarantees you'll find one there to get back.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which night should you go to Daikoku?",
    a: "Weekends bring the most cars, but also the most closures, especially on Friday and Saturday nights. Weeknights are quieter: fewer people, but less risk of finding the entrance shut.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much does a Daikoku tour cost?",
    a: "From €71 as a passenger in a small group, around €120 to ride in a sports car, and from €467 to drive yourself. Prices checked on November 4, 2026.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do you need a license for a tour?",
    a: "Not as a passenger: your passport is enough. To drive, you need your physical license plus an International Driving Permit (1949 Geneva Convention) or an official Japanese translation, depending on your country. The self-drive tour I found also requires drivers to be 21 or older with 3 years of driving experience.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if Daikoku is closed?",
    a: "Tatsumi PA, closer to central Tokyo, and Umihotaru PA, out in the middle of the bay, are the usual fallbacks. The A PIT Autobacs store in Shinonome also stays open late. Good tours adapt on their own.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Daikoku PA: which JDM tour to book, or go on your own?"
  description="Daikoku PA can only be reached by car. Passenger tour, self-drive tour or rental: checked prices, weekend closures and my advice on how to choose."
  article={{ published: "2026-11-04" }}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Experiences", path: "/en/experiences/" },
    { name: "Daikoku PA", path: "/en/experiences/daikoku-jdm-tours/" },
  ]}
>
  <Hero
    image={heroImg}
    eyebrow="Experiences · Yokohama"
    title="Daikoku PA: go on your own or book a JDM tour?"
    subtitle="Japan's most famous car meet can only be reached by car. Here's how to choose."
    focalPoint="50% 62%"
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-10 pb-8 sm:pt-14 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <a
      href={withBase("/en/experiences/")}
      class="tap-target link-underline reveal inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 font-body text-sm font-medium text-ink-soft"
    >
      <Icon name="arrow-left" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
      All experiences
    </a>

    <MentionAffiliation ids={pageOffers} class="mt-6" />

    <div class="reveal mt-8 space-y-4 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <p>
        Daikoku is the most famous highway parking lot in Japan. At night, enthusiasts park their JDM cars, supercars and
        wildest builds there, just to hang out and show off. The catch: you can only get in by car, from the urban
        expressway. If you're already renting a JDM, drive there yourself: it's the most freedom for the least money.
        Otherwise, a passenger tour is the easiest option, from around €70.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'll be straight with you: I couldn't make it during my trip, the weather had other plans. So I did my homework
        for next time: operators' websites, conditions, travelers' reviews. Everything here was checked on November 4,
        2026.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="reveal mt-4 font-body text-xs text-mist-500">
      In the photo: the Tokyo Drift VeilSide RX-7, spotted one night in Tokyo. Not at Daikoku, then.
    </p>

    <PageToc
      items={[
        { label: "What is it?", href: "#what-is-it" },
        { label: "Closures", href: "#closures" },
        { label: "Comparison", href: "#comparison" },
        { label: "With a rental", href: "#rental" },
        { label: "Passenger tours", href: "#passenger" },
        { label: "Self-drive", href: "#self-drive" },
        { label: "Traps", href: "#traps" },
        { label: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="what-is-it" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">So what exactly is Daikoku?</h2>
    <div class="reveal mt-4 space-y-4 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <p>
        Daikoku PA ("parking area") is a rest stop sitting in the middle of a huge interchange in Yokohama, on Tokyo's
        urban expressway, the Shuto. By day it's an ordinary stop: restrooms, a few shops, people passing through. At
        night, and especially on weekends, it's a different world. Skylines, Supras, RX-7s, Lamborghinis, bosozoku
        builds: everyone brings their best.
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing to get clear: it's not an event. No tickets, no schedule, no program. Some nights the lot is packed;
        others it rains and there's hardly anyone. That's part of the charm, and also why you should know what you're
        getting into before paying for a tour.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="closures" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <div class="reveal rounded-lg border border-racing-500/30 bg-racing-500/5 p-5 sm:p-6">
      <p class="eyebrow flex items-center gap-2 text-racing-500">
        <Icon name="flag" class="h-4 w-4" />
        The catch: weekend closures
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 font-body text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        <p>
          This is what many people only find out on the spot. When too many modified cars show up, especially on
          Saturday nights, the entrance is shut and everyone has to leave. According to the Japanese car press, it
          usually reopens between 1 and 3 a.m. One tour operator even says the lot is closed on Fridays and Saturdays
          from 7:30 p.m. to 2 a.m., and takes its guests elsewhere during those hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          The expressway operator also closes the parking area when heavy snow is forecast: it then serves as a base
          for snow clearing.
        </p>
        <p class="font-medium text-ink">
          Don't panic: good tours have a plan B, usually Tatsumi PA or the A PIT Autobacs store in Shinonome. Just
          check that yours does.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">Three ways to get there</h2>
    <p class="reveal mt-4 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      It all comes down to one question: are you already renting a car during your stay?
    </p>
    <ComparisonTable
      class="mt-6"
      caption="Comparison of the three ways to get to Daikoku PA"
      rowHeader="How"
      columns={[
        { key: "price", label: "Price", emphasis: true },
        { key: "duration", label: "Duration" },
        { key: "role", label: "You are" },
        { key: "group", label: "Private or shared" },
        { key: "cancel", label: "Cancellation" },
      ]}
      rows={[
        {
          title: "With a rental car",
          values: {
            price: "¥27,000 for 7 hours at OnlyJDM, plus fuel and tolls",
            duration: "7 or 24 hours",
            role: "The driver",
            group: "Private",
            cancel: "Depends on the agency",
          },
        },
        {
          title: "Passenger tour",
          values: {
            price: "From €71",
            duration: "2.5 to 4 hours",
            role: "A passenger",
            group: "Small group, private options",
            cancel: "Free up to 24 hours before on GetYourGuide",
          },
        },
        {
          title: "Self-drive tour",
          values: {
            price: "From €467",
            duration: "4 hours",
            role: "The driver, following a lead car",
            group: "Private",
            cancel: "Depends on the offer",
          },
        },
      ]}
      note="Prices checked on November 4, 2026: they vary with the date and the option you pick."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="rental" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">Already renting a JDM? Go on your own</h2>
    <div class="reveal mt-4 space-y-4 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <p>
        That's what I would have done with <a href={withBase("/en/tokyo/only-jdm/")} class="link-underline font-medium text-racing-500">my Evo VIII from OnlyJDM</a> if
        the sky had been kinder. Once you're behind the wheel, Daikoku is just an expressway exit: you show up when you
        want, stay as long as you want, and can hit Tatsumi or Umihotaru the same night.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three things to plan for: the license documents your country requires (no rental without them), ideally an ETC
        card for tolls, and a bit of patience to find a spot on busy nights. It's all covered on my <a href={withBase("/en/driving-in-japan/")} class="link-underline font-medium text-racing-500">Driving in Japan</a> page.
        And once there, keep it cool: no rev-limiter bouncing, no burnouts, park up and enjoy. That's also why the place still exists.
      </p>
    </div>
    <EncartReserver ids={["onlyjdm-location"]} class="mt-6" />
  </section>

  <section id="passenger" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">The easiest: a passenger tour</h2>
      <div class="reveal mt-4 space-y-4 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        <p>
          If you're not renting a car, this is the obvious choice. A guide takes you there, handles the driving and the
          tolls, and knows the fallbacks when Daikoku is closed. Three offers clearly stand out:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 border-l-2 border-mist-200 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Daikoku and Tokyo Drift vibes</strong> (GetYourGuide): the cheapest and
            one of the best rated, 4.8/5 from 725 reviews. Three hours in a small group of 10 people max, in a van or a
            sports car depending on the option, with a stop at A PIT Autobacs. In English.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Tokyo Drift III, by Tokyo Turismo</strong> (GetYourGuide): the most
            booked, with 6,300+ reviews at 4.8/5. You ride in a real sports car: their fleet includes R34 Skylines, R35
            GT-Rs, Evos, a drift Chaser… Three or four people per car, and you can't pick which one, but they drop you
            back at your hotel in central Tokyo.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">JDM night tour with a local guide</strong> (Klook): 4.9/5 from 2,300+
            reviews, 2.5 to 3 hours in a JDM car, with a standard or VIP option.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If I were booking tomorrow for a first time, I'd go with Tokyo Turismo: rolling up to Daikoku in a GT-R is
          part of the dream. If budget matters, the first one does the job very well for about €50 less.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <EncartReserver ids={["daikoku-gyg-tokyo-turismo", "daikoku-gyg-car-culture", "daikoku-klook-nuit"]} class="mt-6" />
  </section>

  <section id="self-drive" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">The wildest: drive a JDM to Daikoku yourself</h2>
    <div class="reveal mt-4 space-y-4 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <p>
        On Klook, one offer puts you behind the wheel of a JDM for four hours, following a lead car: about 80 km, with
        a stop at A PIT Autobacs, the Daikoku meet, a run across the Rainbow Bridge and photos at Tokyo Tower. Fuel and
        tolls included.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rules are strict: 21 or older, 3 years of driving, and your physical license with your passport and an
        International Driving Permit (1949 Geneva Convention), or an official Japanese translation for the few
        countries that need one. No copies, no digital versions.
      </p>
      <p>
        It isn't cheap: from €467. For comparison, OnlyJDM rents the Evo VIII for ¥30,000 for 24 hours, and you keep the
        car all day. The real perk of the tour is not having to deal with the route or the parking.
      </p>
    </div>
    <EncartReserver ids={["daikoku-klook-au-volant"]} class="mt-6" />
  </section>

  <section id="traps" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">Traps to avoid</h2>
    <ul class="reveal mt-4 space-y-3 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Closure nights.</strong> On Friday and Saturday nights, keep the closures
        in mind and check your tour's plan B.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Rain.</strong> The operators say it themselves: the number of cars depends
        on the weather. On a rainy night, the lot can be nearly empty. I'm well placed to tell you about it…
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">The fine print.</strong> On the platforms, some very popular offers are
        actually a club membership: their terms state that the ride, the car and even the stop at Daikoku aren't
        guaranteed. Read the description all the way through before paying.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Other people's cars.</strong> No touching, no leaning, no sitting on them.
        For a close-up photo, ask the owner: most of them love to talk about their car.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Your passport.</strong> Operators ask for it, so keep it on you.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" class="scroll-mt-20 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-8 sm:pb-10 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="reveal font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink sm:text-3xl">FAQ</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
      {faq.map((item) => (
        <div class="reveal">
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-ink">{item.q}</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{item.a}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-10 sm:pb-14 sm:px-8">
    <div class="reveal rounded-lg border border-mist-200 bg-paper-dim/40 p-5">
      <p class="eyebrow text-racing-500">Practical info</p>
      <p class="mt-2 font-body text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Daikoku PA</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 font-body text-sm text-ink-soft">
        <li>A rest area on the Shuto urban expressway, in Yokohama (Tsurumi ward)</li>
        <li>Car access only, free entry</li>
        <li>Meets in the evening, mostly on weekends, with no schedule or program</li>
        <li>Frequent closures on Friday and Saturday nights, and when heavy snow is forecast</li>
        <li>Fallbacks: Tatsumi PA, Umihotaru PA, A PIT Autobacs Shinonome</li>
      </ul>
      <a
        href={mapsHref}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="tap-target link-underline mt-3 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 font-body text-sm font-medium text-racing-500"
      >
        Open in Google Maps
        <Icon name="external-link" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-mist-200 bg-paper-dim/50 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 sm:px-8">
      <p class="eyebrow text-mist-500">Read next</p>
      <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-x-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {[
          { label: "My Tokyo spots", href: withBase("/en/tokyo/") },
          { label: "Where to drive in Tokyo", href: withBase("/en/tokyo/where-to-drive/") },
          { label: "My OnlyJDM rental", href: withBase("/en/tokyo/only-jdm/") },
          { label: "Rent a JDM in Japan", href: withBase("/en/rent-a-car/") },
          { label: "Driving in Japan", href: withBase("/en/driving-in-japan/") },
          { label: "All experiences", href: withBase("/en/experiences/") },
        ].map((l) => (
          <li>
            <a href={l.href} class="link-underline inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-1.5 font-body text-sm font-medium text-ink">
              {l.label}
              <Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
